  "brief_description": "This is one of three sections of a predella, the lowest part of an altarpiece. The main panels of the altarpiece show the Virgin and Child, surrounded by narrative scenes of the lives of the saints. The predella originally showed Christ at the centre with the 12 apostles, his followers who preached his message after his death, but it was cut into three some time before it entered the National Gallery's collection.\n\nThese two men, from the right part of the predella, are saints Andrew and Thaddeus. They are shown as old men with white beards, holding scrolls. The inscription in red between them is Thaddeus's name: S. TADEV",
